    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8d008bb9-df2e-5dae-a2df-3502b9422a12",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 y \\cot{(x )} + \\sin{(2 x )}=0\\,,\\ \\sin x\\ne 0\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 y \\cot{(x )} + \\sin{(2 x )}&=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 y \\cot{(x )}&=- \\sin{(2 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(4 \\cot{(x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{- \\sin{(2 x )}}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=4 \\cot{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=- \\sin{(2 x )}.$",
